---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';
import mcpConfig from '../../samples/mcp-config.json.txt?raw';

const toc = [
   { id: 'connect', label: 'Connect the server' },
   { id: 'first', label: 'Make the first call' },
   { id: 'scan', label: 'Ask for a scan' },
   { id: 'sr', label: 'Ask for a screen reader test' },
   { id: 'prompts', label: 'Prompts that work' },
   { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Reporting rules to give the agent' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Connect an AI agent"
   summary="<p>a11ied includes an MCP server. It gives Claude Code, Cursor, or any other agent that supports MCP the a11ied commands as tools, so the agent can scan a page, drive a screen reader, and look up WCAG while it works. The server runs on your machine and opens no network port.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="connect">Connect the server</h2>
      <p>
         Add a11ied to the MCP configuration your agent reads, then restart the agent.
         <abbr title="Model Context Protocol">MCP</abbr> is the standard that lets an agent
         call outside tools.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{mcpConfig.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Claude Code</dt>
         <dd>Run <code>claude mcp add a11ied -- npx -y a11ied mcp</code>.</dd>
         <dt>Cursor</dt>
         <dd>Put that configuration block in <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>.</dd>
         <dt>Other agents</dt>
         <dd>Any client that starts an MCP server over stdio works. The command is <code>npx -y a11ied mcp</code>.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         If you installed a11ied globally, <code>"command": "a1"</code> with
         <code>"args": ["mcp"]</code> uses that copy.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="first">Make the first call</h2>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Call the a11ied doctor tool and tell me what is ready on this machine.</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>doctor</code> reports which browser a11ied will use and which screen readers
         are set up. Now you and the agent both know what it can run here.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="scan">Ask for a scan</h2>
      <p>Start your development server. Give the agent the exact URL.</p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Check http://localhost:3000/account/settings with a11ied. For each problem, give me the element, the WCAG criterion, and the fix. Then list the criteria I still have to check by hand.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The agent calls <code>check</code>. The result has the axe violations, the criteria
         the page triggers, and <code>nextCommands</code>, the lookups to run next. For a
         scan alone, the agent calls <code>run_axe</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="sr">Ask for a screen reader test</h2>
      <p>
         A scan cannot test focus, dialogs, menus, or error messages. A screen reader session
         can.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Use the a11ied simulated screen reader on http://localhost:3000/checkout. Open the payment dialog and check three things: focus moves into the dialog, the dialog announces its name, and Escape returns focus to the button that opened it. Quote the announcements. Stop the session when you finish.</code></pre>
      <p>The agent uses four tools.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>sr_session</code></dt>
         <dd>Starts and stops the session. For the simulated screen reader it passes <code>target: "virtual"</code>. On a machine where VoiceOver or NVDA is the default, it must also pass <code>allowVirtual: true</code>, which is the agent stating that it knows the result is a simulation.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr_action</code></dt>
         <dd>One step: move, press a key, type, set a checkpoint.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr_expect</code></dt>
         <dd>Checks that something was announced.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr_transcript</code></dt>
         <dd>Returns everything the screen reader said.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         Tell the agent to stop the session. A VoiceOver or NVDA session keeps control of the
         screen reader until something stops it.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="prompts">Prompts that work</h2>
      <p>Name the page, the behavior, and what you want back.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Check a change</dt>
         <dd>"Check each route this branch changes with a11ied and report only new problems."</dd>
         <dt>Test a widget</dt>
         <dd>"Look up the ARIA combobox pattern with a11ied, then test our country picker against its keyboard table."</dd>
         <dt>Work one criterion</dt>
         <dd>"Look up WCAG 4.1.3 with a11ied, read how to test it, and test the save button on /settings."</dd>
         <dt>Narrow a bug report</dt>
         <dd>"A user says the cart total is not read out. Reproduce it with a11ied and quote the transcript."</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="mistakes">Reporting rules to give the agent</h2>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt>"The scan passed, so the page meets WCAG"</dt>
         <dd>A scanner decides part of some criteria and none of them fully. Ask the agent to report scan results and <a href="/test-methods">hand-checked results</a> separately.</dd>
         <dt>"VoiceOver says..." from a simulated run</dt>
         <dd>Every session reports <code>targetType</code>: <code>real</code> or <code>simulated</code>. Ask the agent to include it.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         The <a href="/guides/agent-skill">agent skills</a> write both rules into the agent's
         instructions, so you do not have to repeat them. The
         <a href="/reference/mcp">MCP reference</a> lists every tool.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
